================================================================================
spec_analysis.log -- test_vco (cross-coupled NMOS LC VCO, sky130A)
schematic-agent step 3. Consumer: step 4 (testbench audit) and schematic-sizing.
Baseline column = the Step-4 pre-layout run, reproduced twice, testbench/vco_lc_pre_results.txt
================================================================================

--- 3a  PER-KEY READ ----------------------------------------------------------

KEY 1  OscFreq          Direction RANGE   Value [3.4, 3.6]  Units GHz
  baseline 2.72465 GHz  ->  BELOW the window by 0.675 GHz (-19.9% relative to 3.4)
  Circuit expression:
      f0 = 1 / (2*pi*sqrt(L_diff * C_diff))
      L_diff = 2 * 3.9860 nH  = 7.972 nH   (the two coils are in series differentially)
      C_diff = C_node / 2                  (the two half-tanks are in series differentially)
    Back-solving the measured f0 gives C_diff = 428 fF, i.e. C_node = 856 fF, and that
    number reconciles device-by-device with the netlist:
      XC0 MiM 16x16 um   carea=2.00 fF/um^2 * 256 + cperim  ~ 524 fF   (61%)
      coil Cpb           (the output-side half of C_sub)      146 fF   (17%)
      XN2 varactor       Cox ~8.32 fF/um^2 * (40 * 0.5)      ~166 fF   (19%)
      XN0/XN1 Cgd+Cdb+Cgs and wiring                          ~20 fF   ( 2%)
                                                        total ~856 fF   <- matches
    So the C budget is UNDERSTOOD, not guessed, and the dominant term is the fixed MiM cap.
  Governing devices: XC0/XC1 (w,l = plate dimensions) primary; XN2/XN3 (w) secondary;
    XL0/XL1 fixed (TG5, not tunable).
  What must change: C_node must fall to 550 fF for 3.4 GHz, 519 fF for 3.5 GHz,
    490 fF for 3.6 GHz -- a drop of 306..366 fF. The MiM pair alone holds 524 fF, so
    the required drop is available inside ONE knob without touching anything else.
  PLAUSIBLE. Aim for the middle of the window (3.5 GHz), not an edge: RANGE keys have
    a two-sided bar and the varactor drags f0 down again when TuningRange is chased.

KEY 2  TuningRange      Direction FLOOR   Value 600          Units MHz
  baseline 226.43 MHz   ->  2.65x SHORT (f_lo 2.67404, f_hi 2.90047 GHz)
  Circuit expression:
      TR = f(Vcont=VDD) - f(Vcont=0) ;  TR/f0 ~= (1/2) * dC_var / C_tank
    Baseline: TR/f0 = 8.31% -> dC_var/C_tank = 16.6% -> dC_var ~= 142 fF of real swing
    out of C_node = 856 fF. The varactor already swings ~85% of its own 166 fF; the
    problem is NOT that the varactor is weak, it is that it owns only 19% of the tank.
  Required: 600 MHz at 3.5 GHz is TR/f0 = 17.1% -> dC_var/C_tank = 34.3%. At the
    shrunken C_node = 519 fF that is dC_var >= 178 fF.
  Governing devices: XN2/XN3 `w` primary (dC_var scales ~linearly with gate area);
    XC0/XC1 `w,l` equally important but with the OPPOSITE sign -- shrinking the MiM
    raises the varactor's FRACTION without touching the varactor at all.
  THIS IS THE KEY INSIGHT OF THE WHOLE ANALYSIS: shrinking the MiM cap moves OscFreq UP
    and TuningRange UP SIMULTANEOUSLY. The two keys that look like the hardest pair are
    actually aligned on the primary knob, and only the varactor width trades between them.
  PLAUSIBLE. dC_var 142 -> 178 fF is a 1.25x scale on the varactor; even at constant
    varactor width the MiM shrink alone takes dC_var/C_tank from 16.6% to 27%.

KEY 3  OutputPower      Direction FLOOR   Value 10           Units dBm
  baseline 8.66583 dBm  ->  1.334 dB short (A1 = 0.8576 V; the floor needs A1 >= 1.0000 V,
    from dBm = 10*log10(A1^2 / (2*50) / 1e-3), i.e. exactly x1.166 in amplitude)
  Circuit expression:
      A1 ~= (4/pi) * I_tail * R_tank,eff        (current-limited regime)
      R_p,coil = (w0 * L_diff)^2 / R_s,diff = (2*pi*2.7247e9 * 7.972n)^2 / 18.45 = 1010 ohm
    But (4/pi)*I*R_p,coil = 2.05 V while the measurement is 0.858 V, so the loaded
    R_tank,eff is only ~421 ohm -- the coil is NOT the dominant loss. Roughly half the
    tank Q is being eaten by the rest of the tank, principally the MOS varactors'
    channel resistance in series with their gate capacitance (l=0.5 um is a long,
    low-Q channel) and the MiM cap's own rm3+via3 series R.
  Governing devices: XN4/XN5 (`w` ratio -> I_tail) -- BLOCKED by the Power ceiling;
    XN0/XN1 (`w`,`l` -> gm, must satisfy gm*R_tank > 1 with margin);
    and, indirectly but decisively, XC0/XC1.
  THE FREE LUNCH: R_p,coil scales as w0^2, so moving f0 from 2.7247 to 3.5 GHz raises it
    by x1.650 AT ZERO EXTRA CURRENT. That is +2.17 dB if the loss ratio holds -- more than
    the 1.33 dB needed. OutputPower is therefore expected to be carried by the OscFreq fix
    rather than by current, which is the only reason it does not collide with Power.
  PLAUSIBLE, but CONDITIONAL -- it depends on the tank's non-coil losses not scaling up
    as fast as R_p does. Must be verified by simulation at every iteration, never assumed.

KEY 4  Power            Direction CEILING Value 3.0          Units mW
  baseline 2.87729 mW   ->  PASSES, margin 4.1% (0.123 mW)
  Circuit expression:
      Power = VDD * mean(i(vdd)) = 1.8 V * 1.5985 mA
      i(vdd) = I_tail + Iref = 300uA*(XN4.w/XN5.w) + 300uA = 1.20 + 0.30 = 1.50 mA nominal
      (measured 1.60 mA; the excess is the tail transistor's large-signal modulation --
      an averaged switching current, which is exactly why the deck averages over 40
      periods instead of reading a DC op point.)
  Governing devices: XN4.w / XN5.w ONLY. Iref itself is 300 uA fixed IN THE TESTBENCH
    (`iref VDD Iref dc={iref_val}`) and is NOT a device parameter -- the sizer cannot
    touch it, and must not try.
  THE BINDING CONSTRAINT OF THIS DESIGN. 4.1% margin means the tail mirror ratio is
    effectively frozen upward: any widening of XN4 relative to XN5 breaks this key.
  Note XN0/XN1 and XN2/XN3 sizing is POWER-NEUTRAL: the cross-coupled pair only steers
    a current the tail already sets, and a varactor carries no DC current at all.

--- 3b  TENSIONS AND OVER-CONSTRAINT ------------------------------------------

  T1  OscFreq  vs  TuningRange   -- ALIGNED on the primary knob, opposed on the secondary.
      XC0/XC1 shrink:  f0 UP, TR UP.        (both keys improve together)
      XN2/XN3 widen:   f0 DOWN, TR UP.      (a real trade)
      Two knobs, two keys, opposite signature on one of them -> the system is SOLVABLE,
      not over-constrained. Expected recipe: shrink MiM to place f0, then widen the
      varactor for TR and shrink the MiM again to put f0 back.

  T2  OutputPower  vs  Power     -- the classic oscillator collision, DEFUSED here.
      The textbook lever for OutputPower is I_tail, and Power has only 4.1% headroom,
      so that lever is unavailable. It is not needed: the x1.65 rise in R_p that comes
      free with the OscFreq fix covers the 1.33 dB gap. IF that turns out not to hold in
      simulation, this pair IS over-constrained at Iref=300uA and the finding is a
      re-spec question for the user (raise the Power ceiling, or lower the OutputPower
      floor), NOT something to size around.

  T3  OutputPower  vs  TuningRange -- second-order but real and adverse.
      Widening XN2/XN3 adds low-Q channel resistance in parallel with the tank, which
      lowers R_tank,eff and hence amplitude. So the TuningRange fix mildly fights the
      OutputPower fix. Watch OutputPower whenever the varactor width moves.

  T4  start-up margin -- NOT A SPEC KEY, but a hard precondition for all four.
      gm(XN0,XN1) * R_tank,eff > 1 must hold or the circuit stops oscillating and every
      key becomes meaningless (the measurement returns garbage, not a bad number).
      Shrinking the tank C raises R_p, which helps; shrinking XN0/XN1 hurts.
      TREAT A NON-OSCILLATING ITERATION AS AN INVALID POINT, NOT A POOR SCORE.

  OVER-CONSTRAINED KEYS: none flagged. All four are reachable on this topology by the
  argument above, and the user has independently confirmed a jointly-satisfying point
  exists. The one key with no independent knob is Power, and it currently passes.

--- 3c  WHAT MUST BE SIMULATED AND SAVED --------------------------------------

  ANALYSIS: transient only. There is NO .ac analysis for this design and there cannot
  usefully be one -- an oscillator has no stable small-signal operating point to sweep
  about (its DC solution is the unstable symmetric one). Three `tran 2p 60n` runs are
  required, not one, because TuningRange is defined across a Vcont sweep:
      Vcont = 0        -> f_lo
      Vcont = VDD      -> f_hi
      Vcont = VDD/2    -> OscFreq, OutputPower, Power  (the nominal point)
  DO NOT substitute a .op for Power: the op point solves the symmetric non-oscillating
  state and reports the wrong current.

  MUST BE SAVED / MEASURED per iteration:
      t1, t2         zero-crossings of vdiff, 40 periods apart, TD>=30n  -> OscFreq
      tlo1/tlo2, thi1/thi2   the same at the two Vcont endpoints          -> f_lo, f_hi, TuningRange
      vrms  = RMS(vdiff) over exactly [t1,t2]   -> A1 = sqrt(2)*vrms      -> OutputPower
      iavg  = AVG(i(vdd)) over exactly [t1,t2]  -> Power = |iavg| * VDD
      vdiff, v(voutp), v(voutn) waveform                                   -> waveform hand-off
  The integer-period window is not cosmetic: an average over a non-integer number of
  periods leaks tank ripple straight into both iavg and vrms.

  SETTLING: measurements start at TD=30n out of a 60n run, i.e. the first half of the
  transient is discarded as start-up. At ~2.7-3.6 GHz that is 80-110 periods of settling
  and 40 periods of measurement. If sizing lowers the tank Q enough that start-up takes
  longer, this window must be re-checked, not silently trusted.

  OP-POINT EVIDENCE the sizer should read (step 4b's probe splice): vgs/vds/vth/gm/id of
  XN0, XN1, XN4, XN5 at the symmetric op point, to confirm the tail is in saturation and
  to bound gm for the start-up condition. NOTE that op-point values for XN0/XN1 describe
  the UNSTABLE symmetric state, not the limit cycle -- they are a saturation/headroom
  check and a gm estimate, NOT a prediction of amplitude.

  EXPLICITLY NOT USABLE: .claude/reference/compute_fidelity.py. Its extractor registry
  contains only "ac"; called on this design it raises NotImplementedError. Spec values
  are parsed from testbench/vco_lc_pre_results.txt instead (step 4c's script).

--- 3d  SIZING KNOBS, IN PRIORITY ORDER ---------------------------------------

  1. XC0/XC1  w,l  (TG3, tied)   -- MiM plate. Moves OscFreq UP and TuningRange UP and,
       through R_p, OutputPower UP. Power-neutral. THE PRIMARY KNOB.
       Range: the PDK subckt has NO w/l bins at all (see FLOW NOTE), so the electrical
       limit is soft; the practical floor is a drawable plate (>= ~2x2 um).
  2. XN2/XN3  w    (TG4, tied)   -- varactor width. TuningRange UP, OscFreq DOWN,
       OutputPower slightly DOWN. Power-neutral. THE SECONDARY KNOB.
       Range: nfet_01v8 bin wmax = 100 um per copy at nf=1; past that fold with nf/m.
  3. XN0/XN1  w,l  (TG1, tied)   -- gm for start-up margin and switching sharpness.
       Power-neutral (steers the tail current, does not set it). Keep l at 0.15 (Lmin);
       widening adds tank C, so this is a margin knob, not a performance knob.
  4. XN4/XN5  w    (TG2, UNTIED -- ratio is a knob, resolved as open_question Q2)
       -- the ONLY lever on Power, and it currently has 4.1% margin. Effectively
       one-directional: may be lowered, must not be raised without re-checking Power.
  NOT KNOBS: XL0/XL1 and everything inside vco_spiral_ind (frozen, TG5); Iref
       (a testbench source); VDD (a testbench param).

--- FLOW NOTES (for the user's "where does this flow break" question) ----------
  N1  sky130_fd_pr__cap_mim_m3_1 is a plain .subckt with `w=1 l=1 mf=1` defaults and NO
      binning statement, so run_erc_check.py's `check_model_bin_width` finds no wmax for
      it and cannot guard it. The sizer can legally emit a plate size that is not
      physically drawable, and nothing upstream of DRC will object. It also takes `mf`,
      not `m` -- the netlist's `m=1` on XC0/XC1 is handled by ngspice's generic subckt
      multiplier, not by the model.
  N2  Iref (300 uA) and VDD (1.8 V) live in the TESTBENCH, so the two quantities that
      most directly set Power are outside the sizer's reach by construction. That is
      correct -- they are measurement conditions, not device parameters -- but it means
      Power has exactly ONE device knob (the XN4/XN5 width ratio) and no other.
  N3  There is no phase-noise key. The Ocean source measured PhaseNoise via hbnoise;
      ngspice has neither harmonic balance nor pnoise, so it is absent from the spec
      rather than present and unmeasurable. Correct call, and worth stating: this
      toolchain cannot close an oscillator's most important spec at all.
